<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Czechoslovak War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Seven-Day War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Czechoslovak War, also called the Seven-Day War, was a brief conflict in late January 1919 over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a> Silesia. Czechoslovak troops advanced against Polish forces to gain control of the region's coalfields and its railway, before the Allied powers imposed a ceasefire. In 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conference-of-ambassadors">Conference of Ambassadors</a> divided the region along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a> river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-czechoslovak-war.jpg" alt="Polish–Czechoslovak War" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D T G · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polish-Czechoslovak_war_of_1919.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Cieszyn Silesia, the former Duchy of Teschen, had belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Lands of the Bohemian Crown</a> since 1335. The Austrian census of 1910 found three of its districts, Cieszyn, Bielsko and Fryštát, mainly Polish-speaking and the fourth, Frýdek, mainly Czech-speaking. Its importance lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> basin around Karviná, the Košice-Bohumín Railway linking the Czech lands with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, and the junction at Bohumín. The historian Mary Heimann has suggested that the Czechoslovak leaders refused to divide the region for fear of creating a precedent for the German-speaking Sudetenland. On 5 November 1918 the Polish National Council and the Czechoslovak Committee agreed a provisional line that left Frýdek and a small part of Fryštát to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Czechoslovak forces under Josef Šnejdárek moved in to prevent elections to the Polish Legislative Sejm in the territory. The Polish commander Franciszek Latinik had weaker forces, as the bulk of the Polish army was fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-ukrainian-war">Polish–Ukrainian War</a>. On 23 January 1919, before British, French, Italian and U.S. officers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were given two hours to withdraw to the Biała River; the attack began at 13:00 and took Bohumín, Orlová and Karviná. By 27 January the region was in Czechoslovak hands, and the Poles fell back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and then Skoczów. On 31 January, under pressure from the Entente, the attack on Skoczów was called off. On 26 January Czechoslovak forces had killed 20 Polish prisoners at Stonava, where a monument now stands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Czechoslovak troops withdrew to a new line under the agreement of 3 February 1919 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and the territory was placed under international control. The final division of July 1920 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> one third of the population, 142,000 of 435,000, less than half the territory and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> received the railway, most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines and the Třinec ironworks. Some 140,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were left on the Czech side, and the dispute led to Poland's annexation of Trans-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olza">Olza</a> in 1938.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polish–Czechoslovak War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a series of wars fought by the new Polish Army from 1918 to 1921: the Polish–Ukrainian War from Lwów, the Greater Poland uprising, the <b>Polish–Czechoslovak War</b> over Trans-Olza, the Polish–Lithuanian War, three Silesian Uprisings and, most critical, the Polish–Soviet War (1919–1921), which ended in a decisive Polish victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a brief <b>Polish-Czechoslovak war</b>, it was divided along the Olza in 1920, splitting Cieszyn itself in two.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cieszyn">Duchy of Cieszyn</a></li></ul></section>
